WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.230
Film Courage: When we tell a story, we’re
entering into a contract with the audience?

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.940
Adam Skelter, writer, director, story artist:
Yes, from the video [referring to Adam’s

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:06.940
Youtube channel The Art Of Story].

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.590
Film Courage: Can we talk about that?

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.870
What’s the contract?

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.329
Can we get out of the contract?

00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:15.179
Adam Skelter: Sure…you pay a price for it.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:20.010
So basically when you sit down and say “I’m
going to tell you a story,” you are promising

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:25.529
something.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:29.920
And in the video, I draw the comparison, I
use the metaphor of you’re taking out a

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.410
loan from somebody and what you’re promising
is that you’re going to pay it back and

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:39.000
that they will get the interest for their
investment of attention and attention is the

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.670
currency that they are spending.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:45.649
It’s actually probably the most important
currency (like real-world currency) that we’re

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.800
dealing with is the currency of attention,
especially with the Internet, especially with

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.850
the entertainment industry.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:58.010
Literally entertainment is the industry of
getting people to pay attention.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.809
So what you’re doing, if you’re promising
that it’s going to be worth it, there’s

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:06.460
going to be worth putting their time into
it.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:15.140
Your job as the artist is not only to present
conflict in an interesting way but to make

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.340
it mean something to them and that’s where
it gets very subjective.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.170
That’s why you can have some of the great
storytelling and only a few people will get

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.939
it because it’s relative to like whatever
psychological dynamics or whatever value is

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:28.939
resonate with them.

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:35.540
Film Courage: Can you give me an example of
a story that does pay off where the contract

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:42.360
is fulfilled and someone’s obligation is
paid off and then maybe one that is not fulfilled?

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.840
Adam Skelter: Hmmm…a ton.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.509
What’s a good simple one?

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:53.210
BACK TO THE FUTURE…will he get back to the
future?

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.070
Right there you are making a promise with
that title.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.190
Usually the contract you are making with the
audience is right in the title, it’s right

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.640
in the trailer.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.560
Will he get back to the future?

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.770
You’re promising in some way not only are
you going to answer the question, but that

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.640
you’re going to enjoy the ride and then
it’s going to mean something when he gets

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.040
there and I do think that ultimately he goes
through this whole journey.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.940
It’s Michael J. Fox who’s endlessly entertaining
and then was it Zemeckis [writer/director]?

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.520
Who is just brilliant, like it’s perfectly
directed, it’s so much fun and it’s the

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.390
movie that everybody has seen so I would always
use it as like the perfect example of just

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.940
an entertaining movie.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.270
It’s just a completely entertaining movie.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.820
I think it completely fulfilled the contract.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:43.360
You’re entertained the entire time and then
in the end he gives you exactly what you want

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.980
in a way you don’t expect and it’s fun,
you enjoyed it.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.330
But along the way you’re also learning little
themes like about this high school teenager

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.650
who’s learning to appreciate his family
and learning what his parents went through

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:03.030
and stuff like that, that actually resonates
on kind of a more subconscious level.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:08.010
He begins to develop an appreciation for the
different generations and then how that story

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.290
continues on in his own life.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.100
Film Courage: He’s almost reliving his Dad’s
experience in some sense.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:22.710
He’s becoming like…his Dad was not the
most popular guy in high school and was bullied

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.150
and then he’s experiencing it himself because
he [Marty McFly] stands up to Biff.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.450
Adam Skelter: Then another example (which
I think is actually a really interesting example),

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.670
look at NO COUNTRY FOR OLD MEN.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:42.230
Now the thing I love about The Coen Brothers
is that they are constantly subverting the

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:47.760
audience’s expectations, they’re playing
with it, they play with the contract constantly

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.560
which is where that alienates a lot of people.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.250
But the people who are willing to take the
journey with them they find something incredibly,

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.460
much more rewarding.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.600
With NO COUNTRY FOR OLD MEN, I don’t know
if you read the novel, as well?

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:00.720
Film Courage: I did not.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.060
Adam Skelter: Ohhh….I love Cormac McCarthy
[Written in 2005].

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.500
He’s one of my favorite writers.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:12.420
I always compare him to if like Hemingway
and Faulkner had a baby, it would be Cormac

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.420
McCarthy.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.880
So Llewelyn Moss [Josh Brolin’s character]
he’s kind of the hero, he’s the underdog

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.690
in the story and you’re following this whole
journey and the whole contract is, is it going

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:25.120
to mean something that Llewelyn Moss is going
to find this bag full of millions of dollars,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.010
go on a journey and change his life and everything
is going to be awesome after that?

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.710
We’re going to get into spoilers here [***SPOILER
ALERT***].

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.770
Film Courage: Okay, spoiler alert.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.310
Adam Skelter: Spoiler alert.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.940
So when he goes to this entire journey and
in the novel they also did this as well and

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.750
he gets to the point and the sheriff Tommy
Lee Jones [as character Ed Tom Bell] is following

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:52.660
him around and you’re waiting for this whole
thing to build to this, explosive, climatic

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.790
payoff where it’s Anton Chigurh [played
by Javier Bardem] and they are both well-matched

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.830
and there’s this hero and this villain and
you’re waiting for the big standoff…and

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.570
it doesn’t come.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:10.500
Instead Tommy Lee Jones drives up to a hotel,
he sees just barely the periphery of some

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.070
standoff and Llewelyn Moss is gone.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:17.760
And we never even saw it, we just see that
he’s gone.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:24.010
And immediately, when I first read it, I had
to go back and reread it…like “Did I miss

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:25.010
something?”

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.620
Because my first response was “I just got
cheated.”

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:31.110
They got me to invest in Llewelyn Moss, he’s
making this huge sacrifice, why did he do

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.640
this?

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.670
But it was the fact that he did subvert that
contract, that he did subvert our expectations

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:43.860
and then when you see it in the movie, they
adapted it perfectly, where it’s also, it’s

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:50.450
anticlimactic but it’s the fact that you’re
expecting the climax that created the meaningful

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.060
resonance afterward.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:57.590
Like after that when you start looking at
‘what did it mean that Llewelyn Moss just

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.980
left and it wasn’t a standoff?’

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.710
In fact his wife ends up having to pay the
price as well.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:05.690
She ends up leaving by Anton Chigurh, as well.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.470
And ultimately it’s this question (you know
it goes back to the theme) that is this no

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.860
country for old men?

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:24.780
Like is this some evil that’s ancient that
has no mercy for goodness or kind people?

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:33.400
Ultimately it really comes down to when you’re
paying off a contract, you’re trying to

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:40.800
engage their attention and if you do break
it, you have to have a good reason for it.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:49.669
Art is a bridge, you’re trying to build
across the waters to somebody else’s mind.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:54.270
So ultimately every single artist builds their
bridges differently.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.521
Michael Bay will build his bridge all the
way across so people can kind of step on and

00:06:57.521 --> 00:06:59.010
have a conveyor belt across.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.580
And then Kubrick will build this bridge halfway
across and the audience has to build their

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.760
bridge halfway across and they meet in the
middle.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.310
And then you’ll get someone like Wim Wenders
or someone who takes you on a journey and

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:16.169
you don’t know where it’s going and you’re
pretty much building a bridge to where he

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:18.290
is at.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.960
Everybody decides, every artist decides how
far they want to build their bridge across

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:22.960
the water.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.530
Film Courage: Don’t you think though that
every writer thinks that there is a payoff

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:28.570
[in their movie]?

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:34.810
It makes sense to them, but unfortunately
to an outside view, they may not see that

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:35.810
payoff?

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.310
Adam Skelter: Sure.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.440
I can’t speak for other writers.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.650
Sure.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.420
I do think that there is the basic economy
of story, if you promise something give it

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:54.110
in a way that…the old adage is give the
audience what they want in a way they don’t

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.110
expect.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:56.300
But it really comes down to the needs of the
story.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.840
What are you trying to convey with your story?

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.960
And I don’t like imposing too much…like
to me, what’s really interesting is when

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:10.370
finding the exception of those rules until
it actually means something new.

